{
  "book": "GEN",
  "chapter": 40,
  "resources": {
    "costumbres": {
      "c-58bc83a055198a98": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-74",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "It is quite common in the East to carry burdens on the head. Thus the head and neck become so strong that it is not uncommon for a man to carry a weight which requires the united strength of three men to lift from the ground. Women and children, as well as men, carry loads in thi…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-74",
          "sourceHash": "dbb2e499aa81a14238a953c0210a333fd26a91d479ce1eb9a1e226fa09355032",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-76a1e12eebce86ef": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-72",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "According to the Eastern custom, the state-prison formed a part of the dwelling-house of the chief of the executioners, or of some other prominent personage. See Jeremiah 37:15. Sometimes even the king’s palace was so used. See Jeremiah 32:2.",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-72",
          "sourceHash": "9a94a2418bcb13d32610cc4dd69ec372bbee20a60bdba3cc25d9ce88f172f3a3",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-dba04165551c0191": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-73",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "It has been supposed by some that the ancient Egyptians drank no wine, though they did not object to drinking the unfermented juice of the grape, and this text is referred to as an illustration. It was evidently a part of the duty of Pharaoh’s butler to press the grapes into the…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-73",
          "sourceHash": "e96751560fc749b7e6c6ed1b188090f1b6b75b2b095930990a8e1ce20fe16a90",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-ed10e4ddfb677361": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-75",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The Eastern kings celebrated their birthdays by holding feasts and granting pardon to offenders. On the occasion referred to in the text the king availed himself of this custom to pardon the chief butler; although, for some reason not stated, he refused to grant the same clemency…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-75",
          "sourceHash": "5c2f0941f24fc0da8d5caeac5dc503695929a96d8e3eb09969cc50b013505473",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      }
    },
